From fb757e30c45f217e97911c002a2c8a5c85e76d0d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 22:38:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 08/12] util/os_time: sleep with usleep on any POSIX-lite
 platform
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

os_time_sleep falls back to usleep under DETECT_OS_POSIX, while the
includes that make it usable — <unistd.h> and friends at the top of the
file — are already selected under DETECT_OS_POSIX_LITE. A platform that
is POSIX-lite therefore got the headers and then hit "#error Unsupported
OS" a few lines later.

DETECT_OS_POSIX_LITE defaults to DETECT_OS_POSIX, so every platform that
took this branch before still takes it, byte for byte. It only widens to
the POSIX-lite ones, which is what the include block above already
assumed.

usleep is the minimum a platform needs to be POSIX-lite in this file's
terms; the branch above still gives Linux, Managarm and Fuchsia
clock_nanosleep.

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 3 item 5 (timers / clocks) — a DEVIATION from
  this session's item 3 scope, recorded in STATUS.md. It is one line
  and it is what stops src/util compiling.
Why: newlib has usleep and the headers the file wants, but Horizon is
  POSIX-lite, not POSIX (no syslog, no shm, no loader), so the existing
  condition excluded it.
Evidence: building src/util for aarch64/horizon, with
  DETECT_OS_HORIZON in place, still fails with
    os_time.c:99:4: error: #error Unsupported OS
Upstream: yes — POSIX implies POSIX_LITE, so no platform's behaviour
  changes.
---
 src/util/os_time.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/util/os_time.c b/src/util/os_time.c
index 71fc7e3..4660145 100644
--- a/src/util/os_time.c
+++ b/src/util/os_time.c
@@ -86,7 +86,7 @@ os_time_sleep(int64_t usecs)
    time.tv_nsec = (usecs % 1000000) * 1000;
    while (clock_nanosleep(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, 0, &time, &time) == EINTR);
 
-#elif DETECT_OS_POSIX
+#elif DETECT_OS_POSIX_LITE
    usleep(usecs);
 
 #elif DETECT_OS_WINDOWS
